Voltage, current, power bases and units (@pe-basis: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @electrical-power-engineering, #source "../sources/AC-Electrical-Circuit-Analysis-Fiore.lingua#r_0C4FYP5P0A5GQP13HAD8FTEEWK", #source "../sources/Introduction-to-Electric-Power-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_6JR10ZVGMZ7GT4RRK7734JQ7WD", #electrical-power-engineering "../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Energy-power-and-capacity.lingua#r_0EE7P9TMJK8SRVCVM1M7K61RAR") { r_0HB9Q8TC9SKNWV1EAT1CNG97Z6
**Read:** [AC Electrical Circuit Analysis — James M. Fiore](../sources/AC-Electrical-Circuit-Analysis-Fiore.lingua#r_0C4FYP5P0A5GQP13HAD8FTEEWK), sinusoidal quantities and AC power; [Introduction to Electric Power Systems — MIT 6.061](../sources/Introduction-to-Electric-Power-Systems-MIT.lingua#r_6JR10ZVGMZ7GT4RRK7734JQ7WD), network and polyphase quantity conventions.

Write units and the basis of every value: DC or AC, RMS or peak, phase-to-neutral or phase-to-phase, single-phase or total three-phase power, rated or measured, instantaneous or averaged. A 400 V line-to-line system and a 400 V phase-to-neutral model are different systems. Treating them as interchangeable changes current, insulation and fault calculations.

Use W for active power, var for reactive power under a declared sinusoidal convention, VA for apparent power and J or Wh for energy. Prefixes, ratios and powers of ten matter: one kWh is 3.6 MJ, and a 1 kW load running for 3 h uses 3 kWh before auxiliary/loss effects. Energy consumption cannot be inferred from a power rating without time and actual load.

State voltage/frequency bases and tolerance/uncertainty separately. A nameplate rating is a specified capability under conditions, not a measured operating value or a universal limit in another installation. The shared units and energy Records provide the general accounting; power engineering adds connection, waveform, temperature, duty and apparatus conditions. Convert every input to one consistent basis before solving a network or comparing a result with a requirement.

**Continue:** [Energy, power, capacity, and a production denominator](../../../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Energy-power-and-capacity.lingua#r_0EE7P9TMJK8SRVCVM1M7K61RAR).

